<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="scim"
      toc={[
        {"enable", "Enabling directory sync"},
        {"providers", "Provider guides"},
        {"role-mapping", "Mapping groups to roles"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="scim">
        <:dek>
          Directory sync (SCIM 2.0) lets your identity provider push the user lifecycle to emisar:
          provision people from the directory, and when someone is offboarded there, their emisar
          access is revoked automatically — sessions ended, API keys revoked, no manual cleanup.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Set up SSO login first">
        <p>
          Directory sync builds on a configured SSO connection. If you haven't connected your IdP
          yet, start with <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Single sign-on</.link>, then enable directory sync on that connection.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="enable">Enabling directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On a configured connection, enable <strong class="text-zinc-200">directory sync</strong>
        to let your IdP push the user lifecycle to emisar over SCIM 2.0 — the standard Okta, Entra,
        and JumpCloud all speak as a provisioning client. The directory-sync panel lives on each
        connection's page, right under its settings. <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-directory-sync.webp"
        title="emisar console · Directory sync (SCIM)"
        caption="A configured connection: the SCIM base URL to copy, token rotation, and the three steps for pointing your IdP at it."
        alt="The Directory sync (SCIM) panel on a configured connection: an enabled badge, Rotate token and Disable buttons, the SCIM base URL with a copy button, and the expanded 'Point your IdP at this connection' guide — enable SCIM provisioning at your provider, set that base URL as the connector's endpoint and paste the bearer token as its API token, and map the SCIM externalId to the same value the OIDC sub claim carries."
      />

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Click <span class="text-zinc-300">Enable directory sync</span> on the connection.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              emisar shows you two things to wire into your provider's SCIM connector — both visible
              in the panel above:
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  the <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
                  — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/scim/v2</code>
                  on your emisar host (copy button beside it), and
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  a <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>, shown
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">once</strong>
                  when you enable or rotate it. Like every emisar secret it's write-only after that
                  — copy it then. Didn't catch it? Use
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotate token</strong>
                  to mint a fresh one (the old one stops working).
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Point your IdP's SCIM connector at that base URL and bearer.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In your provider, set the SCIM endpoint to the base URL and paste the token as the API
              token — emisar accepts it in the
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Authorization</code>
              header. Enable Create / Update / Deactivate. The connection's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">"Point your IdP at this connection"</strong>
              guide names the per-provider specifics. Expect provisioning to live in its
              <em>own</em>
              app: Okta's OIDC login app can't do SCIM at all, and an Entra app registration can't
              provision either. JumpCloud is the exception — one custom application can carry both.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Match the SCIM externalId to the OIDC subject.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Configure your IdP so the SCIM
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
              carries the same stable identifier as the connection's identifier claim. Then a
              member's SSO login and their synced directory record resolve to one emisar identity —
              no email-matching anywhere. If the two sides can't be made to share an identifier,
              that connection can't be reconciled this way.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Okta</strong>
                  usually lines up untouched: it populates
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  with the Okta user id, and ID tokens from its <em>org</em>
                  authorization server carry that same value as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. Okta documents
                  that in examples rather than as a guarantee, and a custom authorization server
                  lets an admin rewrite the subject claim.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Entra</strong>
                  needs both sides changed: remap
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  from its default
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mailNickname</code>
                  to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">objectId</code>, and set
                  this connection's identifier claim to
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
                  — Entra's <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
                  is per-application, so the login app and the provisioning app would disagree.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-2">
              Whatever the provider, check both values against one real user before you roll out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Once it's wired, here's what the sync does:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
            creates the user and their membership at the connection's default role — so account
            access is in place before their first login. Runner access follows the connection's
            Default runner access setting, which starts at <em>no runners</em>: a provisioned member
            sees the fleet only once you grant it, per member or by group mapping.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Deprovisioning</strong>
            — when the IdP marks a user inactive or removes them — <strong class="text-zinc-200">suspends
            the member</strong>: their sessions are killed and their API keys revoked immediately,
            so an offboard in your directory revokes emisar access automatically. It does
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">not</strong>
            delete the user — the audit trail is preserved. Reactivation in the directory restores
            access.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-synced-users.webp"
        title="emisar console · Synced users"
        caption="Once your IdP is connected, provisioned members appear here with the directory id they came from and the role their groups earned them."
        alt="The Synced users list on a connection: four members provisioned over SCIM, each with a SCIM badge, the directory externalId they were provisioned under, when they last synced, a locked role chip showing the role their group mapping earned them, and a Suspend action."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider guides</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The IdP-side walkthrough lives on each provider's guide, beside its sign-in setup:
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li id="okta" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Okta</.link>
            — SCIM rides its own Okta app, separate from the sign-in integration.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="entra" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Microsoft Entra</.link>
            — a separate enterprise application, with
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
            remapped to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">objectId</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="jumpcloud" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >JumpCloud</.link>
            — the same custom application that signs people in.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="keycloak" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Keycloak</.link>
            — no outbound SCIM; the guide covers what that leaves you to do.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="google-workspace" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Google Workspace</.link>
            — no outbound SCIM; route it through an IdP to close the offboarding gap.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="role-mapping">Mapping directory groups to roles</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        With directory sync on, map an IdP group (by its SCIM <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>) to an emisar role
        — <strong class="text-zinc-200">viewer</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">operator</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">billing manager</strong>,
        or <strong class="text-zinc-200">admin</strong>. A member in several mapped groups gets the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">highest</strong>
        role mapped to any of them.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Owner is never assignable through
        sync</strong>
        — it stays a deliberate human assignment.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-group-role-mapping.webp"
        title="emisar console · Role mapping"
        caption="Each mapping names a directory group by its externalId and the role its members land at. A synced group you don't map leaves its members at the connection's default role."
        alt="The Role mapping panel on a connection: two mappings — the Platform Engineers directory group (externalId kc-grp-platform) mapped to Admin, and SRE On-call (kc-grp-sre) mapped to Operator — each with Edit and Delete, plus an Add mapping button."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM bearer token is an admin-grade credential</strong>
            — it can provision and deprovision across the account. Treat it like a password, and
            rotate it from the connection's panel if it's ever exposed (your IdP loses access until
            you paste the new one).
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Every directory-sync action lands in the
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >
              audit log
            </.link>
            with its authentication method, so you can see that the directory, not a person,
            suspended an offboarded member.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
